# ★★★ THE BUILD ROOT, AS A FILE RATHER THAN AS FOLKLORE.
#
#     The build root used to be a chroot on the Pi, created once by hand and never written down.
#     That was survivable until the Pi became the machine we WIPE to test fresh installs: two
#     formats in one evening took the chroot, the signing key and the source with them, and the
#     rebuild-from-memory shipped 3.0.0-2 with the settings wizard compiled out, because
#     libncursesw-dev was not on the list anyone remembered. A build environment that only exists
#     in somebody's head is one bad evening from shipping a broken release. (2026-08-08)
#
# ★★ BOOKWORM, AND THAT IS THE ENTIRE POINT. CPack derives Depends: from whatever the build
#    machine links against, so building on trixie stamped `libc6 (>= 2.38)` and made the package
#    uninstallable on Debian 12 and Ubuntu 22.04 — it cost a user their working OpenWebRX. glibc
#    is backward compatible, so a binary built against 2.36 runs on 2.41 but never the reverse.
#    Do not "update" this to a newer base image.
#
# ★ arm64 IS built from this file natively on Apple Silicon again (2026-10-25, the M5 Pro — linux/arm64 in colima).
#   10-18 → 10-25 it was cross-built on the Lenovo (Dockerfile.cross-arm64), which is now the VIBE_ARM_ON_LENOVO=1 backup.
# ★★ amd64 AND i386 FROM THIS SAME FILE, on the Lenovo (scripts/build-debs-remote.sh): `--platform
#    linux/386` takes bookworm's i386 image and every library below for i386, natively — an x86-64
#    CPU runs 32-bit code directly. ONE recipe for every architecture, so the i386 package cannot
#    drift from amd64's library list. (uname -m in that container still says x86_64 — CMake keys
#    32-bit x86 off the pointer size for that reason; see VIBE_X86_32.)
FROM debian:bookworm-slim

# ★★★ EVERY ONE OF THESE IS LOAD-BEARING, and the ones that look optional are the dangerous ones:
#     CMake does not fail without them, it QUIETLY DROPS THE FEATURE and prints a status line.
#       libncursesw5-dev  → the settings wizard. Without it `vibeserver` starts a daemon instead
#                           of the setup screen, which is the one command new owners are told to
#                           type. This is what 3.0.0-2 shipped without. Linked STATICALLY now
#                           (libncursesw.a + libtinfo.a): the terminfo database it reads is
#                           ncurses-base, which is Essential, so the package names no ncurses at all.
#     VIBESERVER_STRICT_RADIOS=ON now turns the silent ones into hard errors, but the real fix is
#     that the list lives here instead of in a memory.
# ★★★ NO DISTRIBUTION RADIO OR CODEC LIBRARIES (2026-10-18). librtlsdr-dev, libusb-1.0-0-dev,
#     libopus-dev and libhackrf-dev are GONE from this list, deliberately: build-deps.sh below builds
#     libusb/libopus/librtlsdr from pinned source, and libhackrf/libairspy/libairspyhf compile from the
#     vendored tree. A -dev package left here is a library CMake could quietly link instead — and
#     dpkg-shlibdeps would put it straight back into Depends. (libfftw3-dev went too: nothing has
#     linked FFTW since the DSP moved to PFFFT.) bzip2 unpacks the libusb release tarball.
RUN apt-get update && apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends \
      build-essential cmake pkg-config file ca-certificates curl bzip2 \
      libncursesw5-dev \
      dpkg-dev apt-utils gnupg git rsync xz-utils \
      python3-minimal \
      sdcc \
    && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*
# ★★ python3-minimal IS NOT OPTIONAL SCENERY: scripts/build-mapdata-deb.sh reads the map data's own
#    index.json to decide what goes in the optional detail package. bookworm-SLIM has no python3 and
#    no jq, and a package list parsed with grep is exactly the thing that ships a dataset missing a
#    layer. ~30 MB in a build image, against a file list that cannot drift from the data.

# ★★★ sdcc BUILDS THE RSP BRIDGE FIRMWARE (OpenRSP, 2026-10-21): vendor/openrsp/firmware/src is 8051 C,
#     compiled here into the three images the bundled RSP service uploads. A host tool, architecture-free;
#     CMake refuses a release build without it. bookworm's 4.2.0 reproduces OpenRSP's released images
#     byte for byte (SHA-256 checked against openrsp_3.15.2-20_arm64.deb).
# ★★ The SDRplay headers are NO LONGER STAGED here: the API headers are OpenRSP's, in vendor/openrsp/include.

# ★★★ OUR OWN libusb, libopus AND librtlsdr — PINNED SOURCE, STATIC, INTO A PREFIX OF OUR OWN.
#     Debian's librtlsdr is 2.0.2 and does not know the RTL-SDR Blog V4L ("V4 Lite", R828S) at all;
#     waiting for a distribution to ship a driver means hardware support arrives on THEIR release
#     cycle rather than ours. That was the first library we took over (2026-08-15, shipped as a .so in
#     /usr/lib/vibeserver). The rest followed on 2026-10-18 (Stuart): the package needs nothing from
#     the system but glibc — see build-deps.sh for the pins, the no-udev libusb, and why librtlsdr is
#     now static rather than a private .so.
# ★ /opt/vibe-deps, never /usr: it cannot be confused with, or overwrite, a system copy, and CMake
#   takes EVERY path from it (VIBE_LINUX_DEPS) — a missing file is a configure error, not a fallback.
# ★ Same versions as vibeserver/mac/build-deps.sh, verified inside the script (SHA-256 for the
#   tarballs, the full commit for rtl-sdr, the V4L strings, no udev symbols).
COPY build-deps.sh /opt/build-deps.sh
# ★ Two compile jobs, niced: the Mac overheats and the Lenovo is a live receiver.
ARG DEPS_JOBS=2
# ★ build-debs-remote.sh passes DEPS_NICE=19 on the Lenovo (a live receiver); 10 and DEPS_JOBS=8 on the Mac.
ARG DEPS_NICE=10
RUN JOBS=${DEPS_JOBS} nice -n ${DEPS_NICE} bash /opt/build-deps.sh /opt/vibe-deps native

WORKDIR /work
